## Circuit Breaker States

```
     CLOSED (normal)
     All requests pass through
     Track failure rate over sliding window
           ↓ (failure rate > threshold)
     OPEN (fail fast)
     All requests immediately return fallback
     No calls to downstream service
           ↓ (after waitDurationInOpenState)
     HALF_OPEN (testing)
     Let permittedNumberOfCallsInHalfOpenState through
     If success rate > threshold → CLOSED
     If failure rate > threshold → OPEN again
```

## Resilience4j Setup

```xml
<!-- pom.xml -->
<dependency>
    <groupId>io.github.resilience4j</groupId>
    <artifactId>resilience4j-spring-boot3</artifactId>
</dependency>
<dependency>
    <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
    <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-aop</artifactId>
</dependency>
```

```yaml
# application.yml
resilience4j:
  circuitbreaker:
    configs:
      default:
        slidingWindowSize: 10              # Evaluate last 10 calls
        minimumNumberOfCalls: 5            # Min calls before circuit can open
        failureRateThreshold: 50           # Open if >50% fail
        waitDurationInOpenState: 10s       # Stay open for 10s before half-open
        permittedNumberOfCallsInHalfOpenState: 3  # 3 test calls in half-open
        recordExceptions:                  # Which exceptions count as failures
          - java.io.IOException
          - java.util.concurrent.TimeoutException
          - feign.FeignException.ServiceUnavailable
        ignoreExceptions:                  # Don't count as failures
          - com.example.NotFoundException  # 404 is not a circuit issue
    instances:
      paymentService:
        baseConfig: default
        waitDurationInOpenState: 30s       # Payments need longer recovery time
      inventoryService:
        baseConfig: default
        failureRateThreshold: 30           # Open sooner for critical inventory

  retry:
    instances:
      paymentService:
        maxAttempts: 3
        waitDuration: 500ms
        enableExponentialBackoff: true
        exponentialBackoffMultiplier: 2    # 500ms, 1s, 2s

  timelimiter:
    instances:
      paymentService:
        timeoutDuration: 3s                # Timeout after 3 seconds
```

## Service with Circuit Breaker

```java
@Service
@Slf4j
public class OrderService {

    private final PaymentServiceClient paymentClient;
    private final InventoryServiceClient inventoryClient;
    private final CachedInventoryRepository cachedInventory;

    // Circuit breaker + retry + timeout stacked:
    @CircuitBreaker(name = "paymentService", fallbackMethod = "processPaymentFallback")
    @Retry(name = "paymentService")
    @TimeLimiter(name = "paymentService")
    public CompletableFuture<PaymentResult> processPayment(PaymentRequest request) {
        return CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(() ->
            paymentClient.charge(request)
        );
    }

    // Fallback — must have same return type, plus Throwable param:
    private CompletableFuture<PaymentResult> processPaymentFallback(
            PaymentRequest request, Throwable ex) {
        log.warn("Payment service unavailable: {}. Queuing payment.", ex.getMessage());

        // Option 1: Queue for async retry
        paymentQueue.add(request);
        return CompletableFuture.completedFuture(
            PaymentResult.pending("Payment queued for processing")
        );

        // Option 2: Return error to user (worse UX)
        // return CompletableFuture.completedFuture(PaymentResult.failed("Service unavailable"));
    }

    // Circuit breaker for read operations (cache fallback):
    @CircuitBreaker(name = "inventoryService", fallbackMethod = "getInventoryFallback")
    public InventoryStatus getInventory(String productId) {
        return inventoryClient.getStatus(productId);
    }

    private InventoryStatus getInventoryFallback(String productId, Throwable ex) {
        log.warn("Inventory service down, using cache for {}", productId);
        return cachedInventory.getLastKnownStatus(productId)  // Stale data better than error
            .orElse(InventoryStatus.unknown(productId));
    }
}
```

## Observing Circuit Breaker State

```java
@RestController
@RequestMapping("/admin/circuit-breakers")
public class CircuitBreakerController {

    private final CircuitBreakerRegistry registry;

    @GetMapping
    public Map<String, CircuitBreakerInfo> getAll() {
        return registry.getAllCircuitBreakers().stream()
            .collect(Collectors.toMap(
                CircuitBreaker::getName,
                cb -> CircuitBreakerInfo.builder()
                    .state(cb.getState().name())
                    .failureRate(cb.getMetrics().getFailureRate())
                    .numberOfBufferedCalls(cb.getMetrics().getNumberOfBufferedCalls())
                    .numberOfFailedCalls(cb.getMetrics().getNumberOfFailedCalls())
                    .build()
            ));
    }

    @PostMapping("/{name}/force-open")
    public void forceOpen(@PathVariable String name) {
        registry.circuitBreaker(name).transitionToForcedOpenState();
        // Useful for maintenance or testing fallback behavior
    }
}
```

## Production Reality

Few developers think about circuit breaker configuration tuning. In production, `slidingWindowSize=10` with `minimumNumberOfCalls=5` means it needs 5+ calls to even evaluate. If your service handles 1 req/sec, the circuit won't respond to failures fast enough. Tune based on actual traffic. Also: always implement the fallback method — an open circuit without a fallback just throws a `CallNotPermittedException` which is as bad as the original failure for your users.
